    </title>

    <section xml:id="number-systems">
        <title>
            Number Systems
        </title>
    
        <introduction>
            <p>Number systems, <m>\mathbb{N}</m>, <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>, <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>, <m>\mathbb{R}</m></p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="integers">
            <title>
                The Integers <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>
            </title>

            <p>The integers are the basic number system we are all familiar with, positive and negative whole numbers, and zero. We can solve any additive equation in this system.</p>
        
            <principle>
                <title>
                    Additive Equations
                </title>
            <!-- <idx><h>Induction</h><h>first principle of</h></idx> -->
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For any integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, we can always solve the equation <md>x + a = b</md> for the unknown integer <m>x</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </principle>

            <example xml:id="additive-equations">
                <p>
                    The solution to <md>x+1=5</md> is <m>x=4</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The solution to <md>x+4=7</md> is <m>x=3</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The solution to <md>x+12=7</md> is <m>x=-5</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Notice we need negative numbers to be able to solve any addition equation. If we only had positive numbers, then we can only solve equations when <m>a \le b</m>.
                </p>
            </example>

            <example xml:id="unsolvable-linear-equations">
                <p>
                    What about solving the equation <md>3\cdot x + 2 = 16,</md> for an unknown integer <m>x</m>?
                </p>
                <p>
                    This equation cannot be solved: we can subtract <m>2</m> from both sides to get <md>3\cdot x = 14,</md> but there is no integer <m>x</m> that solves this equation since <m>3</m> does not divide <m>14</m>
                </p>
            </example>

            <p>
                <notation>
                    <usage>
                        <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>
                    </usage>
                    <description>
                        The set of <term>rational numbers</term> (i.e., fractions).
                    </description>
                </notation>
            </p>
        </subsection>
        
        <subsection xml:id="rationals">
            <title>
                The Rationals <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>
            </title>
            
            <p>
                Now we can solve linear equations... (almost, multiplication by <m>0</m>...)
            </p>
        </subsection>
        
        <subsection xml:id="reals">
            <title>
                The Reals <m>\mathbb{R}</m>
            </title>
            
            <p>
                We can now solve some equations, like <md>x^2 -3\cdot x + 4 = 2</md> (quadratic equation)
            </p>
        </subsection>
        
        <subsection xml:id="polynomials-as-numbers">
            <title>
                Polynomials as a Number System
            </title>
            <p>
                Is this really appropriate? Might be more confusing than helpful...
            </p>
        
        </subsection>
    </section>

